[2026/05/14 15:49:16] [DEBUG] isgsa.llm: prompt content: {'Introduction': 'You are an expert recommender systems researcher conducting a code review. Your task is to evaluate whether the code execution was successful or contains bugs. Focus on identifying execution failures, errors, or other issues that would prevent the code from working properly.', 'Research Task': ' You are an expert recommender systems research assistant who is looking to help the user with their requests.\nThe user has some idea and you want to conduct creative experiments to gain scientific insights.\nYour aim is to run experiments to gather sufficient results to report back to the user.\nThe idea is:\n\nAnalyze the provided MovieLens100k dataset u.data (Format: user_id | item_id | rating | timestamp) to identify user behavior patterns.\nConfirm the total number of unique user_ids and unique item_ids identified in the file.\nIdentify the Top 3 most active users (by count of Ratings). For the #1 most active user, calculate their mean rating score.\nIdentify Item 50 and Item 181. Calculate the "co-occurence": How many users rated both items?\nAre there any timestamps that fall outside the expected range for this 1997-1998 dataset?\nDo not guess. Use the full context provided.', 'Implementation': "```python\nimport os\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport lenskit  # kept for compatibility; not heavily used in this baseline\n\n# Setup working directory\nworking_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), 'working')\nos.makedirs(working_dir, exist_ok=True)\n\n# Path to the MovieLens 100k data file\ndata_path = 'u.data'\n\n# 1) Robust data loading with 4 columns strictly enforced\ntry:\n    df_raw = pd.read_csv(\n        data_path,\n        sep=None,\n        engine='python',\n        header=None,\n        on_bad_lines='skip'  # gracefully skip unparsable lines\n    )\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    raise SystemExit(f'ERROR: data file not found: {data_path}. Expected 4 columns: user_id, item_id, rating, timestamp')\nexcept Exception as e:\n    raise SystemExit(f'ERROR loading data: {e}')\n\nif df_raw.shape[1] < 4:\n    raise SystemExit(f'ERROR: Expected at least 4 columns, found {df_raw.shape[1]} columns.')\n\n# Use first 4 columns strictly\ndf = df_raw.iloc[:, :4].copy()\ndf.columns = ['user_id', 'item_id', 'rating', 'timestamp']\n\n# Coerce to numeric, drop rows with NaN in critical columns\nfor col in ['user_id', 'item_id', 'rating', 'timestamp']:\n    df[col] = pd.to_numeric(df[col], errors='coerce')\nna_before = df.isna().any(axis=1).sum()\ndf = df.dropna()\nna_after = df.isna().any(axis=1).sum()\nskipped = na_before - na_after\nif skipped > 0:\n    print(f'Skipped {skipped} rows due to NaN values.')\n\nn_rows = len(df)\nprint(f'Loaded {n_rows} valid rows with 4 columns: user_id, item_id, rating, timestamp')\n\n# 2) Unique counts\nn_users = df['user_id'].nunique()\nn_items = df['item_id'].nunique()\nprint(f'Unique users: {n_users}, Unique items: {n_items}')\n\n# 3) Top-3 active users by rating count with deterministic tie-breaking by user_id\nuser_counts = df.groupby('user_id').size().reset_index(name='count')\ntop3 = user_counts.sort_values(['count', 'user_id'], ascending=[False, True]).head(3)\ntop3_users = top3['user_id'].astype(int).tolist()\ntop3_counts = top3['count'].tolist()\nprint('Top 3 active users (user_id: count):', list(zip(top3_users, top3_counts)))\n# Mean rating for top-1 user\nif len(top3_users) > 0:\n    top1 = int(top3_users[0])\n    mean_top1 = df.loc[df['user_id'] == top1, 'rating'].mean()\n    print(f'Top1 user {top1} mean rating: {mean_top1:.4f}')\nelse:\n    print('No users found for top3 calculation.')\n\n# 4) Co-occurrence for items 50 and 181 with existence checks\nitems_exist = {50: False, 181: False}\nif (df['item_id'] == 50).any():\n    items_exist[50] = True\nif (df['item_id'] == 181).any():\n    items_exist[181] = True\nco_occurrence = 0\nif items_exist[50] and items_exist[181]:\n    users_50 = set(df.loc[df['item_id'] == 50, 'user_id'])\n    users_181 = set(df.loc[df['item_id'] == 181, 'user_id'])\n    co_occurrence = len(users_50 & users_181)\nelse:\n    print('Co-occurrence skipped due to missing item(s).')\nprint(f'Co-occurrence (users who rated both item 50 and 181): {co_occurrence}')\n\n# 5) Timestamp range check (1997-1998)\nif df.empty:\n    print('No data for timestamp analysis.')\nelse:\n    dt = pd.to_datetime(df['timestamp'], unit='s', utc=False)\n    years = dt.dt.year\n    min_year = int(years.min()) if not years.empty else None\n    max_year = int(years.max()) if not years.empty else None\n    out_of_range_mask = (years < 1997) | (years > 1998)\n    n_out = int(out_of_range_mask.sum())\n    print(f'Timestamp range: min_year={min_year}, max_year={max_year}')\n    if n_out > 0:\n        sample = df.loc[out_of_range_mask, ['user_id', 'item_id', 'rating', 'timestamp']].head()\n        print(f'Found {n_out} out-of-range timestamps. Sample rows:\\n{sample}')\n    else:\n        print('All timestamps fall within 1997-1998 range.')\n\n# 6) Simple baseline model: user/item bias with SGD, 80/20 split, deterministic seed\nratings_df = df[['user_id', 'item_id', 'rating']].copy()\nunique_users = np.sort(ratings_df['user_id'].unique())\nunique_items = np.sort(ratings_df['item_id'].unique())\nuser_to_idx = {uid: idx for idx, uid in enumerate(unique_users)}\nitem_to_idx = {iid: idx for idx, iid in enumerate(unique_items)}\nratings_df['u_idx'] = ratings_df['user_id'].map(user_to_idx)\nratings_df['i_idx'] = ratings_df['item_id'].map(item_to_idx)\n\nN = len(ratings_df)\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)  # deterministic seed\nperm = rng.permutation(N)\ntrain_size = int(0.8 * N)\ntrain = ratings_df.iloc[perm[:train_size]].reset_index(drop=True)\nval = ratings_df.iloc[perm[train_size:]].reset_index(drop=True)\n\nn_users_tot = len(unique_users)\nn_items_tot = len(unique_items)\nglobal_mean = train['rating'].mean() if train.shape[0] > 0 else 0.0\nu_bias = np.zeros(n_users_tot, dtype=float)\ni_bias = np.zeros(n_items_tot, dtype=float)\n\nlr = 0.01\nn_epochs = 8\ntrain_rmse_history = []\nval_rmse_history = []\n\nfor ep in range(n_epochs):\n    # SGD over training data\n    for r in train.itertuples(index=False):\n        u = int(r.u_idx)\n        i = int(r.i_idx)\n        pred = global_mean + u_bias[u] + i_bias[i]\n        resid = r.rating - pred\n        u_bias[u] += lr * resid\n        i_bias[i] += lr * resid\n    # Evaluate on training set\n    if train.shape[0] > 0:\n        train_pred = global_mean + u_bias[train['u_idx'].values] + i_bias[train['i_idx'].values]\n        train_pred = np.clip(train_pred, 1.0, 5.0)\n        train_rmse = float(np.sqrt(np.mean((train['rating'].values - train_pred) ** 2)))\n    else:\n        train_rmse = 0.0\n    # Evaluate on validation set\n    if val.shape[0] > 0:\n        val_pred = global_mean + u_bias[val['u_idx'].values] + i_bias[val['i_idx'].values]\n        val_pred = np.clip(val_pred, 1.0, 5.0)\n        val_rmse = float(np.sqrt(np.mean((val['rating'].values - val_pred) ** 2)))\n    else:\n        val_rmse = 0.0\n    train_rmse_history.append(train_rmse)\n    val_rmse_history.append(val_rmse)\n    print(f'Epoch {ep+1}: validation_rmse = {val_rmse:.4f}')\n\n# Final predictions for the validation set\nval_final_pred = global_mean + u_bias[val['u_idx'].values] + i_bias[val['i_idx'].values]\nval_final_pred = np.clip(val_final_pred, 1.0, 5.0)\npred_list = val_final_pred.tolist()\nground_truth = val['rating'].values.tolist()\n\n# 7) Save structured results in a single dict\nexperiment_data = {\n    'lenskit_baseline': {\n        'metrics': {'train': train_rmse_history, 'val': val_rmse_history},\n        'losses': {'train': train_rmse_history, 'val': val_rmse_history},\n        'predictions': pred_list,\n        'ground_truth': ground_truth\n    }\n}\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, 'experiment_data.npy'), experiment_data)\nprint('Saved experiment_data.npy with baseline results.')\n\n```", 'Execution Output': '```\nLoaded 100000 valid rows with 4 columns: user_id, item_id, rating, timestamp\nUnique users: 943, Unique items: 1682\nTop 3 active users (user_id: count): [(405, 737), (655, 685), (13, 636)]\nTop1 user 405 mean rating: 1.8345\nCo-occurrence (users who rated both item 50 and 181): 480\nTimestamp range: min_year=1997, max_year=1998\nAll timestamps fall within 1997-1998 range.\nEpoch 1: validation_rmse = 0.9829\nEpoch 2: validation_rmse = 0.9652\nEpoch 3: validation_rmse = 0.9581\nEpoch 4: validation_rmse = 0.9543\nEpoch 5: validation_rmse = 0.9521\nEpoch 6: validation_rmse = 0.9507\nEpoch 7: validation_rmse = 0.9498\nEpoch 8: validation_rmse = 0.9492\nSaved experiment_data.npy with baseline results.\nExecution time: a second seconds (time limit is an hour).\n```', 'Instructions': ['Carefully analyze the execution output for signs of bugs or failures:', '- Syntax errors, import errors, or runtime exceptions', '- Missing required outputs or metrics', '- Execution timeouts or crashes', '- Incorrect or nonsensical results', '', "If there's a bug, provide a clear summary of the issue and suggest how to fix it.", 'If the execution was successful, leave the summary empty.']}
