[2026/05/14 15:51:29] [DEBUG] isgsa.llm: prompt content: {'Instructions': 'You are an expert recommender system researcher conducting a code review for an important experiment. You are provided the research task, the code implementation and the execution output. You must score the code using the following requirements: \nCode implementation requirements for analyzing MovieLens100k u.data:\n- Data loading: read the u.data file as 4 columns: user_id, item_id, rating, timestamp; tolerate tab or whitespace delimiter; no header; enforce numeric dtypes (int, int, float, int).\n- Basic parsing validation: ensure exactly 4 columns; skip/flag non-parsable rows gracefully.\n- Unique counts: compute total number of unique user_ids and unique item_ids present in the dataset.\n- Top activity detection: identify the top 3 most active users by rating count (descending); use deterministic tie-breaking (e.g., smaller user_id) to break ties.\n- Top user metric: for the #1 most active user, compute the mean rating value they assigned.\n- Co-occurrence analysis: item IDs 50 and 181; compute the number of users who rated both items (intersection of user sets for each item).\n- Existence checks: if either item 50 or item 181 has zero ratings, report gracefully and skip co-occurrence calculation.\n- Timestamp handling: convert timestamps to datetime; identify any records with year outside {1997, 1998}; report the count and sample (e.g., up to 5 entries).\n- Range validation criterion: define out-of-range as any timestamp not mapped to year 1997 or 1998; explicitly report such records.\n- Output and reporting: produce a structured result that includes: total unique users, total unique items, top 3 users with counts, mean rating of top user, item co-occurrence count, and out-of-range timestamp details (count and samples).\n- Robustness: code should handle varying whitespace, extra columns defensively, and missing values in a controlled way with clear errors.\n- Performance considerations: leverage vectorized operations (e.g., pandas) to ensure scalability for 100k rows; memory usage should be reasonable.\n- Reproducibility and determinism: results should be deterministic given the dataset; document tie-breaking rules and any random seed usage if applicable.\n- Error handling: provide clear, actionable error messages for file-not-found, unreadable data, or unexpected formats.\n- Assumptions: document data format expectations (4 columns, no header, numeric types, tab/space delimiter).\n- Implementation note: prefer Python with pandas; if another language is used, map these requirements accordingly but ensure the same behavioral outcomes.\n. Each requirement can either be fullfilled or not fulfilled. The score is the percentage of requirements that are fulfilled, e.g. if 7 out of 10 requirements are fulfilled, the score is 70. Additionally, you provide clear and constructive feedback how to improve the code implementation based on the requirements.', 'Research Task': ' You are an expert recommender systems research assistant who is looking to help the user with their requests.\nThe user has some idea and you want to conduct creative experiments to gain scientific insights.\nYour aim is to run experiments to gather sufficient results to report back to the user.\nThe idea is:\n\nAnalyze the provided MovieLens100k dataset u.data (Format: user_id | item_id | rating | timestamp) to identify user behavior patterns.\nConfirm the total number of unique user_ids and unique item_ids identified in the file.\nIdentify the Top 3 most active users (by count of Ratings). For the #1 most active user, calculate their mean rating score.\nIdentify Item 50 and Item 181. Calculate the "co-occurence": How many users rated both items?\nAre there any timestamps that fall outside the expected range for this 1997-1998 dataset?\nDo not guess. Use the full context provided.', 'Implementation': "```python\nimport os\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom datetime import datetime\nimport gzip\nimport zipfile\nimport urllib.request\nimport io\nimport random\n\n# Try to import LensKit; if unavailable, continue with pure pandas-based implementation\ntry:\n    import lenskit as lk  # type: ignore\n    from lenskit import batch  # type: ignore\n    HAVE_LK = True\nexcept Exception:\n    HAVE_LK = False\n\n# Setup working directory as required\nworking_dir = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), 'working')\nos.makedirs(working_dir, exist_ok=True)\n\n# Helper: download and extract ml-100k if missing\nML100K_ZIP_URL = 'https://files.grouplens.org/datasets/movielens/ml-100k.zip'\nZIPPED_NAME = 'ml-100k.zip'\nEXTRACTED_FOLDER = 'ml-100k'\n\ndef download_and_extract_ml100k(dest_dir: str) -> str:\n    zip_path = os.path.join(dest_dir, ZIPPED_NAME)\n    if not os.path.exists(zip_path):\n        print('Downloading MovieLens 100k dataset (ml-100k.zip)...')\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(ML100K_ZIP_URL) as resp:\n            data = resp.read()\n        with open(zip_path, 'wb') as f:\n            f.write(data)\n        print('Download complete.')\n    else:\n        print('ml-100k.zip already present, skipping download.')\n\n    # Extract if not already extracted\n    extract_target = os.path.join(dest_dir, EXTRACTED_FOLDER)\n    if not os.path.isdir(extract_target):\n        print('Extracting ml-100k.zip...')\n        with zipfile.ZipFile(zip_path, 'r') as zf:\n            zf.extractall(dest_dir)\n        print('Extraction complete.')\n    else:\n        print(' ml-100k already extracted.')\n    return os.path.join(dest_dir, EXTRACTED_FOLDER)\n\n# Locate data: try common paths; if missing, attempt download\ndef load_u_data_from_paths(extract_root: str) -> pd.DataFrame:\n    # possible relative paths after extraction\n    candidates = [\n        os.path.join(extract_root, 'u.data'),\n        os.path.join(extract_root, 'u.data.gz'),\n        os.path.join(extract_root, 'ml-100k', 'u.data'),\n    ]\n    for p in candidates:\n        if os.path.isfile(p):\n            if p.endswith('.gz'):\n                with gzip.open(p, 'rt') as f:\n                    df = pd.read_csv(f, sep='\\t', header=None, names=['user_id','item_id','rating','timestamp'])\n            else:\n                df = pd.read_csv(p, sep='\\t', header=None, names=['user_id','item_id','rating','timestamp'])\n            return df\n    return None\n\n# Ensure data is loaded\n\ndef ensure_dataset() -> pd.DataFrame:\n    # First, search in current working dirs for ml-100k/u.data\n    possible_paths = [\n        os.path.join(os.getcwd(), 'ml-100k', 'u.data'),\n        os.path.join(os.getcwd(), 'datasets', 'ml-100k', 'u.data'),\n        os.path.join(os.getcwd(), 'ml-100k', 'u.data.gz'),\n    ]\n\n    df = None\n    for p in possible_paths:\n        if os.path.isfile(p):\n            if p.endswith('.gz'):\n                with gzip.open(p, 'rt') as f:\n                    df = pd.read_csv(f, sep='\\t', header=None, names=['user_id','item_id','rating','timestamp'])\n            else:\n                df = pd.read_csv(p, sep='\\t', header=None, names=['user_id','item_id','rating','timestamp'])\n            break\n\n    if df is None:\n        # Attempt to download and extract ml-100k\n        extract_root = download_and_extract_ml100k(working_dir)\n        df = load_u_data_from_paths(extract_root)\n        if df is None:\n            raise FileNotFoundError('Failed to load u.data from downloaded dataset. Ensure network access.')\n\n    # Ensure correct dtypes\n    df['user_id'] = df['user_id'].astype(int)\n    df['item_id'] = df['item_id'].astype(int)\n    df['rating'] = df['rating'].astype(float)\n    df['timestamp'] = df['timestamp'].astype(int)\n    return df\n\n# 1) Load dataset with robust fallback\nprint('Loading MovieLens 100k data...')\ndf = ensure_dataset()\nprint('Data loaded. Shape: ', df.shape)\n\n# 2) Unique counts\nn_users = df['user_id'].nunique()\nn_items = df['item_id'].nunique()\nprint(f'Total unique users: {n_users}')\nprint(f'Total unique items: {n_items}')\n\n# 3) Top 3 most active users by rating count\nuser_counts = df.groupby('user_id').size().sort_values(ascending=False)\ntop3 = user_counts.head(3)\nprint('Top 3 most active users (user_id -> count):')\nprint(top3)\nif not top3.empty:\n    top1 = top3.index[0]\n    top1_mean = df.loc[df['user_id'] == top1, 'rating'].mean()\n    print(f'Top-1 user: {top1}, mean rating: {top1_mean:.4f}')\nelse:\n    top1 = None\n    top1_mean = None\n\n# 4) Co-occurrence for item 50 and 181\ndef cooccurrence(df, a, b):\n    users_a = set(df.loc[df['item_id'] == a, 'user_id'])\n    users_b = set(df.loc[df['item_id'] == b, 'user_id'])\n    return len(users_a & users_b)\n\ncooc_50_181 = cooccurrence(df, 50, 181)\nprint(f'Co-occurrence (users who rated both 50 and 181): {cooc_50_181}')\n\n# 5) Timestamps outside 1997-01-01 to 1998-12-31 inclusive\n\ndef is_out_of_range(ts):\n    try:\n        dt = datetime.utcfromtimestamp(ts)\n        year = dt.year\n        return year < 1997 or year > 1998\n    except Exception:\n        return True\n\nout_of_range_mask = df['timestamp'].apply(is_out_of_range)\nout_of_range = df.loc[out_of_range_mask]\nprint(f'Number of timestamps outside 1997-1998 range: {len(out_of_range)}')\nif len(out_of_range) > 0:\n    mins = out_of_range['timestamp'].min()\n    maxs = out_of_range['timestamp'].max()\n    print(f'Time range outside: min_ts={mins}, max_ts={maxs}')\n\n# 6) Simple baseline: User mean predictor with 5 random train/test splits, track RMSE\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\nrandom_seeds = [42, 7, 123, 999, 2024]\nrmse_list = []\ntrain_rmse_list = []\nfor epoch, seed in enumerate(random_seeds, start=1):\n    train_df, test_df = train_test_split(df, test_size=0.2, random_state=seed)\n    global_mean = train_df['rating'].mean()\n    user_means = train_df.groupby('user_id')['rating'].mean()\n    def predict(row):\n        u = row['user_id']\n        if u in user_means:\n            return user_means[u]\n        else:\n            return global_mean\n    predictions = test_df.apply(predict, axis=1)\n    rmse = np.sqrt(np.mean((test_df['rating'] - predictions) ** 2))\n    rmse_list.append(rmse)\n    # training RMSE on train set for completeness\n    train_preds = train_df.apply(lambda r: user_means.get(r['user_id'], global_mean), axis=1)\n    train_rmse = np.sqrt(np.mean((train_df['rating'] - train_preds) ** 2))\n    train_rmse_list.append(train_rmse)\n    print(f'Epoch {epoch}: validation_rmse = {rmse:.4f}, training_rmse = {train_rmse:.4f}')\n\n# 7) Save metrics and data\nexperiment_name = 'movielens_100k_baseline'\nexperiment_data = {\n    experiment_name: {\n        'metrics': {'train': train_rmse_list, 'val': rmse_list},\n        'losses': {'train': train_rmse_list, 'val': rmse_list},\n        'predictions': [],\n        'ground_truth': [],\n    }\n}\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, 'experiment_data.npy'), experiment_data)\nprint('Saved experiment data to:', os.path.join(working_dir, 'experiment_data.npy'))\n\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, 'rmse_per_epoch.npy'), np.array(rmse_list))\nnp.save(os.path.join(working_dir, 'train_rmse_per_epoch.npy'), np.array(train_rmse_list))\n\n```", 'Execution output': '```\nLoading MovieLens 100k data...\nml-100k.zip already present, skipping download.\n ml-100k already extracted.\nData loaded. Shape:  (100000, 4)\nTotal unique users: 943\nTotal unique items: 1682\nTop 3 most active users (user_id -> count):\nuser_id\n405    737\n655    685\n13     636\ndtype: int64\nTop-1 user: 405, mean rating: 1.8345\nCo-occurrence (users who rated both 50 and 181): 480\nNumber of timestamps outside 1997-1998 range: 0\nEpoch 1: validation_rmse = 1.0417, training_rmse = 1.0297\nEpoch 2: validation_rmse = 1.0465, training_rmse = 1.0285\nEpoch 3: validation_rmse = 1.0545, training_rmse = 1.0265\nEpoch 4: validation_rmse = 1.0400, training_rmse = 1.0301\nEpoch 5: validation_rmse = 1.0435, training_rmse = 1.0292\nSaved experiment data to: C:\\Users\\lukas\\AutoRecLab_Team7\\workspace\\working\\experiment_data.npy\nExecution time: 2 seconds seconds (time limit is an hour).\n```'}
